---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const orbitCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'r', label: 'orbital radius r', min: 6600, max: 50000, step: 100, value: 6800, unit: 'km' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'orbital speed √(GM/r)', expr: 'sqrt(398600/r)', unit: 'km/s' },
    { label: 'orbital period 2πr/v', expr: '2*3.1416*r/sqrt(398600/r)/60', unit: 'min' },
    { label: 'needed centripetal a = v²/r', expr: '398600/(r*r)', unit: 'km/s²' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/circular-motion-and-gravitation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Move in a circle and you are <strong>always accelerating</strong> — not because your speed
    changes, but because your direction does. The acceleration points at the centre:
    a = v²/r. Something must supply it (a string, friction, a seat, gravity), or you exit on a
    tangent. The profound consequence: <strong>an orbit is free-fall that keeps missing</strong> —
    gravity bends your path exactly as fast as you travel forward.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Earth-orbit calculator — drag the altitude and watch speed drop while period balloons" widget="sliderLab" config={orbitCfg} />

  <p>
    At 200 km altitude (r ≈ 6,600 km from Earth's centre) the speed is 7.8 km/s and the trip
    takes 90 minutes — the space station's life. Slide out to geostationary distance (r ≈ 42,000
    km) and the required speed falls to 3.1 km/s, with a period of exactly 24 hours: the
    satellite hangs over one spot. The readouts use GM⊕ = 398,600 km³/s².
  </p>

  <h2>Gravity as the universal glue</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Newton's synthesis:</strong> the same law — F = Gm₁m₂/r² — pulls apples and moons. The falling Moon <em>is</em> the falling apple, 60 Earth-radii farther out and 3,600× weaker (60²), which matches observation.</li>
    <li><strong>Circular motion in life:</strong> corners, curves and spin cycles are all v²/r demands. Take a curve twice as fast and the needed friction quadruples.</li>
    <li><strong>Weightlessness explained:</strong> astronauts aren't beyond gravity (gravity at 400 km is ~89% of surface strength) — they and their ship fall together, so nothing pushes on them.</li>
    <li><strong>Tides are the differential:</strong> the Moon pulls harder on Earth's near side than its far side; the stretch, not the pull, raises the oceans twice daily.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Newton, Principia; OpenStax, College Physics (Uniform Circular Motion and Gravitation).</p>
</TopicPage>
